# Keep the frontend a strict typed single-page application

# Frontend structure

The default frontend is a Vite-built React single-page application separated from the FastAPI
backend. Server-side rendering and Next.js are forbidden. Do not add server-framework routing or a
second API layer. Use the configured client router for links,
navigation, location, lazy route loading, and not-found behavior.

Dependencies flow downward:

`pages -> components -> services -> lib`

- Pages compose routes and data orchestration.
- Components own reusable presentation and interaction; primitives live under `components/ui/`.
- Services own typed API calls.
- `lib` owns the shared client, storage, configuration, formatting, and pure helpers.
- Contexts provide cross-route session and locale state; hooks contain reusable interaction logic.

Pages never call `fetch` directly and components never import backend implementation modules. Use
strict TypeScript with no `any`; derive API types from the generated contract. Prefer functional
components, composition, local state for local concerns, and small custom hooks over prop drilling or
global catch-all state.

Use PascalCase component files and `<Capability>Page.tsx` route pages. The initial home surface may
show project intent and primary domain, but must not expose a business action that has no accepted PRD
and implementation.

Lazy-load route modules and genuinely heavy components. Add memoization only for measured expensive
work or a stable context value, not as a default ritual.

The only full-stack UI exception is a Streamlit application whose accepted PRD limits the entire UI
to prompt input, chat responses, and conversation history. Do not use this exception for forms,
dashboards, administration, or a general-purpose business UI, and do not mix Streamlit into the
React/FastAPI tree. Such work starts from the separately identified `chat-only-fullstack` mode in
`technology-profile.yml`. Chainlit and other full-stack UI frameworks are not baseline dependencies.
